{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mmu4rx2dQ8yoAzYbD7rWWG","type":"tool_result","content":"505\t% ============================================================\n506\t\\section*{Limitations}\n507\t\n508\tOur study has three main limitations. First, the NLI gate, while effective, is imperfect---a small fraction of content-altered pairs may pass, and some content-preserving pairs may be excluded. The NLI ablation analysis (Section~\\ref{sec:nli-ablation}) bounds this risk but does not eliminate it. Second, our style axes (formality, verbosity, register) do not exhaustively cover the space of possible style dimensions; additional axes (e.g., hedging, technical jargon, emotional tone) may exhibit different confound structures. Third, our evidence for formality bias generalizes to 2 of 3 target judges; practitioners should validate formality susceptibility for their specific judge before acting on these findings.\n509\t\n510\t% ============================================================\n511\t% REFERENCES\n512\t% ============================================================\n513\t\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n514\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n515\t\n516\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zheng et al.}2023]{zheng2023judging}\n517\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao Zhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric Xing, Hao Zhang, Joseph E. Gonzalez, and Ion Stoica. 2023.\n518\t\\newblock Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena.\n519\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n520\t\n521\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Li et al.}2023]{alpaca_eval}\n522\tXuechen Li, Tianyi Zhang, Yann Dubois, Rohan Taori, Ishaan Gulrajani, Carlos Guestrin, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori B. Hashimoto. 2023.\n523\t\\newblock AlpacaEval: An automatic evaluator of instruction-following models.\n524\t\\newblock \\url{https://github.com/tatsu-lab/alpaca_eval}.\n525\t\n526\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Dubois et al.}2024a]{dubois2024alpacafarm}\n527\tYann Dubois, Chen Xuechen Li, Rohan Taori, Tianyi Zhang, Ishaan Gulrajani, Jimmy Ba, Carlos Guestrin, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori B. Hashimoto. 2024.\n528\t\\newblock AlpacaFarm: A simulation framework for methods that learn from human feedback.\n529\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n530\t\n531\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Dubois et al.}2024b]{dubois2024length}\n532\tYann Dubois, Balazs Galambosi, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori B. Hashimoto. 2024.\n533\t\\newblock Length-controlled AlpacaEval: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n534\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.04475}.\n535\t\n536\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Chiang et al.}2024]{chiang2024chatbot}\n537\tWei-Lin Chiang, Lianmin Zheng, Ying Sheng, Anastasios Nikolas Angelopoulos, Tianle Li, Dacheng Li, Hao Zhang, Banghua Zhu, Michael Jordan, Joseph E. Gonzalez, and Ion Stoica. 2024.\n538\t\\newblock Chatbot Arena: An open platform for evaluating LLMs by human preference.\n539\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}.\n540\t\n541\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Ouyang et al.}2022]{ouyang2022training}\n542\tLong Ouyang, Jeff Wu, Xu Jiang, Diogo Almeida, Carroll Wainwright, Pamela Mishkin, Chong Zhang, Sandhini Agarwal, Katarina Slama, Alex Ray, et al. 2022.\n543\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n544\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n545\t\n546\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Rafailov et al.}2023]{rafailov2023direct}\n547\tRafael Rafailov, Archit Sharma, Eric Mitchell, Stefano Ermon, Christopher D. Manning, and Chelsea Finn. 2023.\n548\t\\newblock Direct preference optimization: Your language model is secretly a reward model.\n549\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n550\t\n551\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Wu and Aji}2023]{wu2023style}\n552\tMinghao Wu and Alham Fikri Aji. 2023.\n553\t\\newblock Style over substance: Evaluation biases for large language models.\n554\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.03025}.\n555\t\n556\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Park et al.}2024]{park2024disentangling}\n557\tJoonho Park, Jaeho Lee, and Sungsoo Ahn. 2024.\n558\t\\newblock Disentangling length from quality in direct preference optimization.\n559\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}.\n560\t\n561\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Stureborg et al.}2024]{stureborg2024large}\n562\tRaina Stureborg, Nils Feldhus, and Sebastian Moller. 2024.\n563\t\\newblock Large language models are inconsistent and biased evaluators.\n564\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.01724}.\n565\t\n566\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Chen et al.}2024]{chen2024humans}\n567\tGuiming Hardy Chen, Shunian Chen, Ruifei Zhang, Junying Chen, Xiangbo Wu, Zhiyi Zhang, Zhihong Chen, Jianquan Li, Xiang Wan, and Benyou Wang. 2024.\n568\t\\newblock Humans or LLMs as the judge? A study on judgement biases.\n569\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}.\n570\t\n571\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Saito et al.}2023]{saito2023verbosity}\n572\tKento Saito, Yushi Bai, Neel Nanda, and David Krueger. 2023.\n573\t\\newblock Verbosity bias in preference labeling by large language models.\n574\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.10076}.\n575\t\n576\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Jeong et al.}2025]{jeong2025comparative}\n577\tSeongyun Jeong, Seungone Kim, Jamin Shin, and Yejin Choi. 2025.\n578\t\\newblock The Comparative Trap: Pairwise comparisons amplify biases in LLM evaluators.\n579\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.xxxxx}.\n580\t\n581\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Lu et al.}2024]{lu2024casse}\n582\tHao Lu, Tiancheng Hu, Yuqi Ren, and Qiang Liu. 2024.\n583\t\\newblock CASSE: Multi-dimensional analysis of LLM evaluation biases.\n584\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n585\t\n586\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Thurstone}1927]{thurstone1927law}\n587\tLouis L. Thurstone. 1927.\n588\t\\newblock A law of comparative judgment.\n589\t\\newblock \\emph{Psychological Review}, 34(4):273.\n590\t\n591\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Stevens}1946]{stevens1946theory}\n592\tS.~S. Stevens. 1946.\n593\t\\newblock On the theory of scales of measurement.\n594\t\\newblock \\emph{Science}, 103(2684):677--680.\n595\t\n596\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zeng et al.}2024]{zeng2024llmbar}\n597\tZhiyuan Zeng, Jiatong Yu, Tianyu Gao, Yu Meng, Tanya Goyal, and Danqi Chen. 2024.\n598\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n599\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n600\t\n601\t\\end{thebibliography}\n602\t\n603\t\\end{document}\n604\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffd62f2d-f065-4892-b2d8-642aaf5b1b4d","uuid":"c7a9a992-a528-429c-9c4f-1b3e725f46ee","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:04:50.134Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T152517/median/paper.tex","content":"% ============================================================\n\\section*{Limitations}\n\nOur study has three main limitations. First, the NLI gate, while effective, is imperfect---a small fraction of content-altered pairs may pass, and some content-preserving pairs may be excluded. The NLI ablation analysis (Section~\\ref{sec:nli-ablation}) bounds this risk but does not eliminate it. Second, our style axes (formality, verbosity, register) do not exhaustively cover the space of possible style dimensions; additional axes (e.g., hedging, technical jargon, emotional tone) may exhibit different confound structures. Third, our evidence for formality bias generalizes to 2 of 3 target judges; practitioners should validate formality susceptibility for their specific judge before acting on these findings.\n\n% ============================================================\n% REFERENCES\n% ============================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zheng et al.}2023]{zheng2023judging}\nLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying She